#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106964} :activity/id #uuid "55ebad96-168a-5d79-a4bb-de542d4a2040" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608 :topic/id #uuid "0465e4ab-285c-57c0-b16a-07caca22daf3" :topic/math-academy-id 1352} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105838 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105671 :question/id #uuid "08cf33de-c71e-55bb-8906-112075c06a37" :question/math-academy-id "e-7691"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85c79564-93e7-5054-9534-71dc8dc54f7b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048921}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508962}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Rectangle or a Square Given Its Perimeter"} :step/id #uuid "470f5ea9-9e6d-525a-95cd-a5d8c2257844" :step/math-academy-id 28598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107049 :tutorial/content "The **area** of a shape is a measure of how much space is enclosed by a shape on a plane. Let's consider a square with sides of length of $1\\,\\textrm{in}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc59b6e5a60a2f22e6c7fb102eba2f255dd09e115fd04a16050570f9f8b053f4.png)\n\nThe area of this square is equal to ${\\color{red}{1\\,\\textrm{in}^2}},$ which we say in words as \"one square inch.\"\n\nSimilarly, if we have three squares with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ and $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ respectively, then their respective areas will be \n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2, \\qquad\n\t1\\,\\textrm{m}^2, \\qquad \n\t1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b7b7096-09d8-55d7-a7bc-45c3a83c9412" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2302 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b19100e-d452-53e4-9fd7-da598e7cbf2e" :step/math-academy-id 9594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107050 :tutorial/content "Let's now suppose that we would like to compute the area of the square $ABCD,$ shown below. Notice that, this time, the sides of the square span multiple unit lengths.\n\n![](images/14/1441893036f9a41ed0daa8c8ce078d6a733bab5bb88dc60259a6b9ebc1c2e7c0.png)\n\nThe length of one side of a grid square is equal to $1\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ So, the area of one grid square is $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find how many grid squares are contained inside $ABCD.$\n\n![](images/5f/5f293762b05013689996298a7804fb5f507eb228112397641fb8f662ff74ba49.png)\n\nInside the large blue square, there are $3$ rows containing $3$ grid squares each, so we can fit exactly $3 \\times 3 = 3^2 = 9$ grid squares inside $ABCD.$\n\nEach grid square has area $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2,$ so the area $\\mathcal A$ of $ABCD$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = (3\\,\\textrm{cm})^2 = 9\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.\n$$\n\nIn general, if we have a square with a side of length $a$, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5e28b50-44b1-59d2-a7d6-e3ffb7a10038" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2303 :tutorial/title "The Area of a Square"} :step/id #uuid "e7fe8167-acaa-551f-8d92-567afcc40716" :step/math-academy-id 9595 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106967}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105534 :question/id #uuid "f7dc8ebd-817d-5709-99a5-f21dc726c2d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2936"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "305f1d10-f98a-5849-a566-549265b3508d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050792}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508971}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Square From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "fc7ddcc6-0df2-5e0c-8bc0-d2a0e1080407" :step/math-academy-id 9597 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107052 :tutorial/content "In general, for a rectangle with sides of length $a$ and $b,$ the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b.\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate by finding the area of the rectangle $PQRS,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0890441b4c7e888ca7ede71a075825ca6700f79d242d1dec7e35ab2d787e5e0.png)\n\nFrom the figure, we see that the rectangle has the following side lengths:\n\n$$\na = {PQ} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n\t\\qquad\n\tb = {PS} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =60\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter which side we call $a$ and which side we call $b.$ In the above example, if we set\n\n$$\na = {QR} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n\\qquad\nb = {RS} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =60\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas before." :tutorial/id #uuid "2dff044c-7f7f-5531-8db6-05e1e8b7be7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2305 :tutorial/title "The Area of a Rectangle"} :step/id #uuid "1a603e54-4f54-513f-82c5-b99d25dae778" :step/math-academy-id 9600 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105932 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105537 :question/id #uuid "cf89adb1-3344-5ed4-b2e0-48506af917bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2940"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8e574a9-a37d-512f-aa4d-543ad1a3de84" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508986}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Rectangle From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "adc09c20-5a5a-5c4e-9d39-c6bae768710b" :step/math-academy-id 9601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105538 :question/id #uuid "022da369-7dfd-5865-8e72-4317203ad11e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2941"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0726a51-b1a9-5755-a19b-0cc68f96b875" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508939}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Side of a Rectangle Given Another Side and Its Area"} :step/id #uuid "405a478b-76e6-5357-a2ad-65d8bc8e55d8" :step/math-academy-id 9604 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106796}}] :activity/title "Areas of Rectangles and Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105987}]}
